<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
Agent worktree broker reaper — every 3h at :15 WITA (W62 ANTIBODY #1).
Runbook: docs/runbooks/agent-worktree-broker.md §Auto-cleanup

Invokes the cleanup mode of scripts/agent_start.py (WIP-safe + skip-recent) so
abandoned worktrees no longer accumulate into a storm. Offset to :15 to keep a
pass out of the WR2 worktree-gc (:00) and worktree_gc_universal (:30) slots on
.worktrees/ — that offset is the reason for the odd minute, keep it.

But read that offset for exactly what it is. BOTH cross-family reviewers
(kimi-code/k3 and tp1-qwen3.8-max, 2026-08-31) independently rejected the
earlier wording "never races" as false, and they are right: an offset is
SCHEDULING, not mutual exclusion. It separates two jobs that start on time and
finish quickly; it does nothing if a :00 GC overruns 15 minutes, if a run is
delayed by load or wake-from-sleep, or if someone invokes the cleanup mode by
hand.
Nothing anywhere takes a lock (verified: no flock in the wrapper, none on
agent_start.py's cleanup path). launchd will not run two instances of THIS
label, so self-overlap is not the hazard — crossing the others is, and eight
passes expose that pre-existing race eight times as often as one did.

CADENCE, and why it is no longer daily (2026-08-31): a single daily pass cannot
survive a burst. On the night of 2026-08-30→31 an agent wave minted 40 worktrees
after the 00:15 run had already fired, and the next scheduled pass was 24h away.
The reaper was neither dead nor mis-scheduled — its log carries real removals,
and on the burst it correctly protected every worktree holding unmerged commits,
a live process or WIP — it was simply out-cadenced. Eight passes a day bring the
worst case toward ~3h of accumulation instead of ~24h.

"Toward", not "bound": tp1-qwen3.8-max rejected the stronger word and is right.
The ~3h figure holds only while the machine is awake, each pass starts on time
and finishes quickly. launchd COALESCES calendar firings missed during sleep —
a laptop shut overnight gets one catch-up run, not the several it slept through
— so eight entries mean eight nominal opportunities, never eight guaranteed
passes.

Provenance of those numbers, so a later reader can weigh them. MEASURED in the
2026-08-31 session: the per-checkout size (du -sh, ~1.2 GB each), free space of
753 MB immediately before the recovery pass, and per-worktree ages from the
broker's own AGE_MIN column, printed by its list mode (which showed the bulk
between 4 and 26 hours old).

A WORD ON THE SHAPE OF THIS COMMENT, because it bit twice in one session. XML
forbids TWO CONSECUTIVE HYPHEN CHARACTERS anywhere inside a comment, and also
forbids a comment body that ends on a hyphen. That is the entire rule, and it is
narrower than "avoid dashes": a single-hyphen flag such as -ctime is perfectly
legal in here (this comment names several), while an ASCII double dash used as
ordinary PUNCTUATION is not. The hazard is the character pair, not the fact that
a word happens to be a flag. A cross-family reviewer rejected the first version
of this very paragraph, which told authors to avoid naming flags: it banned
something legal and missed the punctuation case entirely.

The defect this PR cures was exactly that character pair, and both times a
reviewer's correction got written into this header it came back, each time as a
long-form flag name spelled out in full.

The trap that let it through twice: plutil accepts such a file and answers OK,
and its extract mode even reads the correct schedule out of it. Apple's parser —
the one launchd itself uses — does not care, measured rather than assumed, so
the job would have kept running. Python's plistlib raises ExpatError, and
plistlib is what audit_launchd_crons.py uses. The blast radius is therefore the
AUDIT layer going blind, not the cron dying; and the lenient probe is the one an
author reaches for first. Verify this file with plistlib, and scan EVERY comment
rather than the position expat names: it reports only the first offence, so
curing what it points at can leave a second one alive. That is precisely how
this survived its own first correction.

The "40 of 41 inside 24h" count came from `find -ctime -1`, and a cross-family
reviewer was right to flag it: `-ctime` is inode CHANGE time, not birth time
(macOS spells that `-Btime`), so any metadata touch inside the window also
counts. Treat that count as an upper bound corroborated by the AGE_MIN column,
not as a birth-time census. It is left stated rather than quietly re-labelled,
because the number did steer a real decision.

The "~80 GB free the previous evening" figure is the OWNER'S REPORT, not a
reading anyone captured — no log on this fleet records free space at that
moment. Corroborating trace that does exist: ~/logs/disk_watchdog.out.log shows
the Data volume going 85% -> 97% used between 2026-08-30 09:00 and 08-31 09:00.

THE ARITHMETIC DOES NOT CLOSE, and the narrative must not imply it does.
tp1-qwen3.8-max caught this: ~40 checkouts at ~1.2 GB is ~48 GB, while "~80 GB
free -> 753 MB" implies ~79 GB consumed. Worktrees are the LARGEST IDENTIFIED
contributor and the only one this file fixes; they are not the whole drop, and
roughly 30 GB stays unattributed.

Re-measured after the recovery pass, as CURRENT STATE and never as an overnight
delta — nothing snapshotted these paths before the incident, so no delta is
recoverable: .worktrees is 41 GB over 36 directories (1.14 GB average, so the
1.2 GB figure is a fair round number); ~/.ollama holds 49 GB of models; and
~/Desktop/OSINT-Nexus/data/autonomy holds 18 GB with an autonomous writer
confirmed running at the time of measurement. Those last two are the plausible
remainder BY SIZE ONLY — a hypothesis, not a measurement.

One suspect is positively ELIMINATED rather than merely unproven:
/private/var/vm/sleepimage is 26 GB but its mtime is 2026-08-18, thirteen days
earlier, so it consumed nothing that night. It is a standing occupant of the
volume, not a participant in this incident.

The count itself is approximate and moved while the session ran (36 live now,
7 reaped by the recovery pass, 2 created by the session's own work). Cure for
the next occurrence, since the honest answer here is "no baseline existed":
capture a dated `du -sh` of these paths NOW so the next such question is
answered by subtraction instead of by inference.

CLOCK FRAME of the two jobs this offset dodges, verified 2026-08-31 rather than
inherited from the old comment: wr2.worktree-gc fires {Hour 0, Minute 0} and
worktree-gc-universal {Hour 0, Minute 30}, both via StartCalendarInterval, i.e.
both LOCAL like this one. The offset is therefore a real margin in a shared
frame — and the superseded "00:00 UTC" wording was wrong about them too, not
just about this job.

NOTE ON THE LABEL: it still ends in `.daily`, deliberately. The label is an
opaque identifier that connectome probes (docs/connectome/edges/launchd-*.yaml)
grep literally; renaming it to match the cadence would silently stop those
probes matching — the W120 shape, an alarm that zeroes itself in silence. The
cadence is stated here and in the runbook; the identifier stays put.

Install (Pro):
    cp infra/launchagents/com.nuzantara.agent-worktree-cleanup.daily.plist.example \
       ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.nuzantara.agent-worktree-cleanup.daily.plist
    launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.nuzantara.agent-worktree-cleanup.daily.plist
  (or: bash infra/launchagents/install_agent_worktree_cleanup.sh)

Kill switch (without removing plist):
    launchctl setenv AGENT_BROKER_ENABLED false
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.nuzantara.agent-worktree-cleanup.daily</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>/Users/nuzantara/nuzantara/scripts/agent_worktree_cleanup_cron.sh</string>
    </array>

    <!-- launchd reads StartCalendarInterval in LOCAL time (WITA on this fleet),
         NOT UTC. The previous comment here claimed "00:15 UTC = 08:15 WITA";
         measured 2026-08-31 it is the other way round — the wrapper logged
         [2026-08-30T16:15:05Z] for a run the broker stamped 00:15:06+0800, i.e.
         00:15 WITA = 16:15 UTC. Read these hours as WITA. -->
    <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
    <array>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>0</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>15</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>3</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>15</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>6</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>15</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>9</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>15</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>12</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>15</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>15</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>15</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>18</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>15</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>21</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>15</integer></dict>
    </array>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>/Users/nuzantara/nuzantara</string>

    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>AGENT_BROKER_ENABLED</key>
        <string>true</string>
        <key>HOME</key>
        <string>/Users/nuzantara</string>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/bin:/bin</string>
    </dict>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/nuzantara/logs/agent-worktree-cleanup.log</string>

    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/nuzantara/logs/agent-worktree-cleanup.error.log</string>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>

    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
